# Line-ending policy. Added at Phase 9b (2026-08-10).
#
# Why this exists: the project is developed on Windows but every gate runs in a
# Linux container and in CI, and several files are rewritten programmatically by
# the generators and report writers. Without a policy, the same file arrives as
# CRLF from an editor and LF from a script, and Git reports every line of a
# 700-line document as changed when nothing in it actually changed.
#
# That is not a cosmetic annoyance here. `CLAUDE.md` sec.9 makes a reproducible
# artifact part of "publication-ready", and the phase-by-phase commit history is
# part of that claim. A diff where the real change is buried under 843 lines of
# invisible whitespace churn is a history nobody can audit.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: Git decides what is text, normalises it to LF in the
# repository, and checks it out as LF. Modern Windows tooling -- VS Code, Python,
# PowerShell 7 -- handles LF without complaint.
#
# One-time renormalisation after adding this file:
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "chore: normalise line endings to LF"
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary and generated formats that must never be normalised. SVG is text and is
# deliberately left to the rule above, because `reports/figures/*.svg` is
# regenerated by `scripts/run_eda.py` and a meaningful diff on it is the whole
# reason those figures are SVG rather than PNG.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary

# Jupyter notebooks: text, but diff-hostile. Marked so tooling knows what they
# are; `notebooks/01_eda.ipynb` is a thin viewer over tested functions and holds
# no stored outputs, so it stays readable in a diff.
*.ipynb text eol=lf

# Data artefacts. JSONL corpora are gitignored, but provenance and the sampling
# plan are committed and are read by humans in review.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
